// ---------- Long-form guide (paragraph strings may contain inline HTML) ----------

interface GuideTable { caption: string; head: string[]; rows: string[][]; hideSm?: number[] }
interface GuideSection { id: string; heading: string; paragraphs: string[]; list?: string[]; after?: string[]; table?: GuideTable }

const guide: GuideSection[] = [
  {
    id: "who-we-are",
    heading: "Who we are and how remote delivery works for Australian clients",
    paragraphs: [
      "BtechWaleTech is three engineers in India. Ankur Kumar is the full stack developer. Santosh Sharma handles AI and machine learning work, AWS infrastructure, data science, technical SEO and deployment. Vedansh Shrivastava runs project management, data science, AI and automation. There is no sales team, no account manager and no pool of juniors that your project disappears into. The people on your first call are the people who build the site.",
      "We work with Australian businesses remotely, and only remotely. We do not have an office in Sydney or Melbourne, we do not have an ABN, and we do not have staff in Australia. Some offshore providers blur that line with a virtual office address or a local phone number that forwards overseas; we think that is a poor way to start a working relationship, so we state it plainly here and on every page of this site.",
      "Remote delivery works well for web projects because almost everything that matters is visible and checkable online. Within the first week you get a private staging link where the real site is being built, not a set of static mockups. You open it on your own phone, on your office desktop and on the laptop at home, and you comment directly. Every decision is written down: the scope, the page list, what is included, what is excluded and what each milestone costs. If something is said on a call, it is confirmed in writing afterwards so nobody has to rely on memory.",
      "The main adjustment for Australian clients is the time difference, which is smaller than most people expect. India is behind every Australian time zone, so our morning is your afternoon. A typical rhythm is a short call or a written review in your afternoon, then build work through our day while you are out of the office, and a written update waiting when you start the next morning. The time-zone section further down explains the exact overlap for each state, including daylight saving.",
      "We are a good fit for small and mid-sized businesses, startups, sole traders, not-for-profits and in-house marketing teams that need reliable engineering without agency overheads. We are not the right fit if you need someone on site for workshops, if your procurement rules require an Australian entity with local insurance and security clearances, or if the project needs dozens of people at once. When that is the case we will say so on the first call rather than take a job we cannot deliver well.",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "services-detail",
    heading: "Our services for the Australian market, in detail",
    paragraphs: [
      "<strong>Website development.</strong> Business websites, landing pages and content sites built on modern frameworks such as Astro and Next.js, or on WordPress when your team wants to edit pages themselves. For most service businesses we recommend a static site: it loads quickly on a phone with one bar of 4G in a regional town, it is cheap to host, and there is very little to hack. The static plan covers up to 100 pages, responsive layouts, enquiry forms, hosting, SSL and deployment. Everything is written in Australian English, with enquiry rather than inquiry, optimise rather than optimize, prices shown with GST included where you sell to consumers, dates as DD/MM/YYYY and phone numbers formatted the way Australians dial them. The <a href=\"/services/web-development/\">web development service page</a> covers the technical detail.",
      "<strong>SEO websites at scale.</strong> Some businesses genuinely need hundreds of pages: a trade that covers many suburbs, a directory, a comparison site, or a catalogue of products and variations. Our SEO website plan covers 700+ pages built around search intent, with keyword and page planning, schema, a sitemap and internal linking, taken from design to deployment. The key word is genuine. We only plan location pages for places you actually serve and have something specific to say about, because thin copies with the suburb name swapped are exactly what Google's helpful content and spam systems are designed to demote.",
      "<strong>Ecommerce.</strong> Online stores on Shopify, WooCommerce or a custom stack, chosen according to your catalogue, budget and how much control you want. We set up Stripe, Shopify Payments, PayPal, Square, Afterpay and Zip where they suit your platform, configure GST-inclusive pricing and shipping rules that reflect what Australia Post, Sendle or your courier actually charge to regional and remote postcodes, and connect orders to Xero or MYOB. The ecommerce section below goes into payments and delivery in more depth.",
      "<strong>SEO for google.com.au.</strong> Monthly SEO covers technical health, on-page work, content and local search. For businesses that serve a metro area or a region we focus on service pages, suburb pages where they are justified, and Google Business Profile guidance. For national online stores we focus on category and product pages. If you also trade in New Zealand, we set up separate en-AU and en-NZ pages with hreflang so each market sees the right prices and spelling. More on the <a href=\"/services/seo-services/\">SEO services page</a>.",
      "<strong>Custom software and web apps.</strong> Client portals, booking and quoting tools, staff dashboards, job tracking for field teams, membership areas and internal admin panels. A common Australian example is a trades or facilities business that has outgrown spreadsheets but finds off-the-shelf job software too rigid for one part of its workflow. Another is an allied health practice that wants a patient intake form which writes directly into its practice management system. Many requests for a mobile app are better served by a progressive web app that installs from the browser and updates without app store review.",
      "<strong>AI automation.</strong> Automations that remove repetitive admin: website enquiries created as jobs in ServiceM8 or Tradify, paid orders pushed into Xero as invoices, booking confirmations and reminders by SMS or email, lead summaries sent to the right person, and AI assistants that draft replies to common questions or classify incoming requests, with a human checking anything that matters. We map the current process first, because automating a messy workflow just produces mess faster.",
      "<strong>Maintenance.</strong> After hosting goes live you get five months of maintenance included: content and price updates, bug fixes, security updates, backups, uptime and speed checks and basic SEO health checks. After that, maintenance continues from " + usd("Maintenance") + ", or you contact us only when something needs changing. See all starting prices on the <a href=\"/pricing/\">pricing page</a>.",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "ecommerce-australia",
    heading: "Ecommerce in Australia: Stripe, Shopify, PayPal, Afterpay, Zip and delivery",
    paragraphs: [
      "Selling online in Australia has a few features that stores built for other markets often get wrong. Buyers expect prices to include GST, they expect to see delivery costs to their postcode before they reach the last step, many expect a buy now pay later option in fashion, homewares, beauty and electronics, and anyone outside the capital cities has learned to check whether a store actually delivers to them.",
      "<strong>Platform choice.</strong> Shopify suits most small and mid-sized stores: it is managed, secure, has solid Australian payment and shipping apps, and your team can run it without a developer. WooCommerce suits businesses that already run WordPress, want lower monthly platform fees or need unusual product logic. A custom build makes sense for B2B ordering with customer-specific price lists, complex configurators, subscriptions with unusual rules, or very large catalogues where platform limits start to bite. We recommend one for your situation and explain why, rather than using one tool for every job.",
      "<strong>Card payments and wallets.</strong> Stripe, Shopify Payments, PayPal and Square all accept Visa, Mastercard and American Express, and most support Apple Pay and Google Pay, which matter because a large share of Australian online shopping now happens on phones. Fees, settlement times and dispute handling differ by provider and plan, so compare their current terms before you choose. Each merchant account needs business verification and is opened in your name; we never process payments through our own accounts.",
      "<strong>Afterpay and Zip.</strong> Buy now pay later is widely used by Australian shoppers, and both Afterpay and Zip integrate with Shopify, WooCommerce and most custom checkouts. The provider approves your merchant application and sets your fees, which are typically higher than card fees, so it is worth checking whether your margins support them. We add the checkout option, the product-page messaging the provider requires, and test the full flow, including refunds, before launch.",
      "<strong>Bank transfer and PayID.</strong> For B2B stores and high-value orders, bank transfer is still common. PayID makes it easier because customers can pay to an email address or phone number instead of a BSB and account number, and payments usually arrive quickly through the New Payments Platform. We can build order flows that hold stock while waiting for payment and mark the order paid when you confirm receipt.",
      "<strong>Shipping and delivery.</strong> Australia is large, and delivery costs to Perth, Darwin, Tasmania or remote Queensland can be very different from Sydney to Melbourne. We configure shipping rules by postcode or zone, connect Australia Post, StarTrack, Sendle, CouriersPlease or an aggregator such as Shippit where you use them, show delivery estimates honestly, and handle click and collect if you have a shopfront. For bulky or fragile goods we set up quote-on-request flows rather than inventing a flat rate that loses money on half your orders.",
      "<strong>GST, invoices and accounting.</strong> A GST-registered business selling to consumers should show GST-inclusive prices, and customers who ask for a tax invoice should get one that meets ATO requirements. We configure tax settings and invoice templates to your accountant's instructions and connect the store to Xero or MYOB so orders, fees and refunds do not have to be retyped. Please confirm your GST settings and product tax classes with your accountant before launch; we implement them, we do not decide them.",
      "<strong>Consumer law at checkout.</strong> The Australian Consumer Law applies to online stores the same way it applies to shops. Returns and refund pages must not suggest customers have fewer rights than the consumer guarantees give them, prices must be shown as a total rather than drip-fed, and pre-ticked add-ons or hidden subscriptions are exactly what regulators have been pursuing. We write checkout flows and templates that avoid those traps, and you confirm the final policy wording with your adviser.",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "seo-australia",
    heading: "SEO for google.com.au: local search, suburbs and hreflang",
    paragraphs: [
      "Australian search is heavily local. People search for a service plus a suburb, or they tap the map results and call whichever business looks closest and most credible. A plumber in Parramatta, a physio in Fremantle and a family lawyer in Toowong are all competing in different small markets, even though they use the same Google. Good SEO for an Australian business starts by working out which of those small markets you actually want to win.",
      "<strong>Technical foundations.</strong> Every site we build ships with a clean URL structure, one H1 per page, unique titles and meta descriptions, a sitemap submitted in Search Console, canonical tags, correct robots settings, compressed images in modern formats and structured data for your business type. Pages are tested against Core Web Vitals on a throttled mobile connection, because many Australian customers browse on the train, on a job site or in a regional town with patchy coverage.",
      "<strong>Local SEO and Google Business Profile.</strong> For businesses that serve customers in an area, the Google Business Profile often drives more calls than the website itself. We guide you through setting it up or cleaning it up: the most specific primary category, accurate service areas for mobile businesses, hours, services, photos and a website link that goes to the most relevant page. The profile must be owned and verified by you. We also check that your business name, address and phone number read the same on your site, your profile, Yellow Pages, True Local, Hipages, industry directories and social profiles.",
      "<strong>Suburb and service-area pages.</strong> A page for each area you really serve can rank if it says something specific: typical jobs in that area, travel or call-out details, local landmarks customers recognise, response times, or photos of work done nearby. Twenty identical pages with the suburb name swapped usually do not rank and can drag the rest of the site down. We start with your most valuable three to five areas and add more only when there is genuine content for them.",
      "<strong>Content that answers Australian questions.</strong> The pages that win are usually the ones that answer the exact question a customer has. For a trades business that is call-out fees, after-hours availability, licence numbers and what a typical job costs. For a clinic it is fees, gap payments, bulk billing, health fund rebates, parking and what to bring. For an online store it is delivery times to each state, returns and whether Afterpay is available. We plan content around those questions, not around a keyword density target.",
      "<strong>google.com.au and ccTLDs.</strong> Google no longer offers a manual country-targeting setting in Search Console, so it relies on signals such as a .com.au or .au domain, hreflang, Australian addresses and phone numbers where you have them, local currency and content that is clearly written for Australians. A .com.au domain is a strong local signal. If you use a .com instead, hreflang and clearly Australian content matter more.",
      "<strong>hreflang for en-AU and en-NZ.</strong> Businesses that trade in both Australia and New Zealand often run one site with the same pages for both. That works until prices, GST rates, delivery rules and spelling differences start confusing customers. We can build separate en-AU and en-NZ versions, usually in /au/ and /nz/ folders or on .com.au and .co.nz domains, with hreflang tags pointing each version at the other and an x-default for everyone else. Each version gets its own currency, GST treatment and delivery information.",
      "<strong>Reporting.</strong> Monthly SEO starts from " + usd("Monthly SEO") + " and includes a plain report from Search Console: which pages gained impressions and clicks, which queries are growing, what we changed and what we plan next. We do not promise positions, and you should be wary of anyone who does. Rankings move daily; we judge progress month on month.",
